# =============================================================================
# Environment template for the CRM TimeSheet project.
#
# Copy this file to `.env` in the same directory and replace the placeholder
# values. `.env` is gitignored and must NEVER be committed.
#
#   Windows (PowerShell):  Copy-Item .env.example .env
#   Linux / macOS:         cp .env.example .env
#
# Docker Compose reads `.env` automatically because it sits next to
# `docker-compose.yml`. The NestJS backend reads it through @nestjs/config.
#
# The backend validates the variables it uses at startup and refuses to boot
# when one is missing or malformed — see
# `backend/src/config/env.validation.ts`.
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PostgreSQL — consumed by the `postgres` service in docker-compose.yml.
#
# IMPORTANT: POSTGRES_USER, POSTGRES_PASSWORD and POSTGRES_DB are read by the
# postgres entrypoint on FIRST boot only, when it initialises the data
# directory. Changing them later has no effect while the named volume
# `crm-timesheet-postgres-data` still exists — see README "Data persistence".
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Required. Database superuser created on first boot.
POSTGRES_USER=crm_user

# Required. Password for POSTGRES_USER. Use a strong value; never commit it.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=replace_with_strong_password

# Required. Default database created on first boot.
POSTGRES_DB=crm_timesheet

# Optional. Host port the container's 5432 is published on, bound to
# 127.0.0.1 only. Change it if a local PostgreSQL install already owns 5432.
# Defaults to 5432 when unset.
POSTGRES_PORT=5432

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Application
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Required by the backend. PostgreSQL connection string.
# Host is `localhost` because the backend runs on the host during development.
# It becomes `postgres` (the Compose service name) once the backend itself
# runs as a Compose service. Keep user/password/db in sync with the values
# above, and with POSTGRES_PORT if you changed it.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://crm_user:replace_with_strong_password@localhost:5432/crm_timesheet?schema=public"

# Optional. Runtime environment for the backend. One of `development`, `test`
# or `production`. Defaults to `development`.
NODE_ENV=development

# Optional. HTTP port the NestJS backend binds to. Integer between 1 and 65535.
# Defaults to 3000.
PORT=3000

# Optional. Comma-separated list of browser origins allowed to call the API
# (CORS). Each origin must match scheme + host + port exactly — no trailing
# slash, no path:
#
#   CORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173,https://crm.example.com
#
# `*` allows any origin; credentials are then disabled automatically, because
# browsers reject credentialed requests against a wildcard.
#
# When unset or empty, no browser origin is allowed — the safe default, so a
# misconfigured environment fails closed. Non-browser clients (curl, Postman,
# server-to-server calls) send no Origin header and are never affected.
#
# The value below is the Vite dev server default, i.e. the local frontend.
CORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Authentication — read by the backend's auth module, and by nothing else.
#
# The two secrets are REQUIRED. Along with DATABASE_URL they are the only
# variables the backend refuses to start without, and unlike the SMTP block
# below there is no degraded state to fall back to: an API that cannot sign a
# token cannot answer a single request.
#
# It refuses rather than doing either of the two obvious things, both of which
# are worse:
#
#   * inventing a secret at boot — every restart would silently log the whole
#     company out, because tokens signed by the previous process are rejected
#     as forgeries;
#   * shipping a default — it would be published in this repository, and anyone
#     could mint a super-admin token against any deployment that did not
#     override it.
#
# Generate each one separately, never by hand:
#
#   Linux / macOS:        openssl rand -base64 48
#   Windows (PowerShell): [Convert]::ToBase64String((1..48|%{Get-Random -Max 256}))
#
# Rotating JWT_ACCESS_SECRET invalidates every access token in flight (people
# are logged out within JWT_ACCESS_TTL). Rotating JWT_REFRESH_SECRET logs
# everybody out immediately — that, together with emptying the `refresh_tokens`
# table, is the lever to pull after a suspected compromise.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Required. Signs access tokens (HS256). At least 32 characters.
JWT_ACCESS_SECRET=replace_with_a_long_random_value_min_32_chars

# Required. Signs refresh tokens (HS256). At least 32 characters, and it MUST
# differ from JWT_ACCESS_SECRET — the backend refuses to start if the two are
# the same. One secret for both would mean a refresh token verifies as an access
# token, so the long-lived credential a client stores would work directly
# against every protected route.
JWT_REFRESH_SECRET=replace_with_a_different_long_random_value

# Optional. Seconds an access token stays valid, 60–3600. Defaults to 900
# (15 minutes).
#
# This is the revocation window and nothing else. An access token is verified
# from its signature alone — no row is read — so it cannot be revoked: an
# account deactivated, a role downgraded or a session logged out is still
# honoured by a token already in flight until it expires. Everything revocable
# lives on the refresh side, in the `refresh_tokens` table.
# JWT_ACCESS_TTL=900

# Optional. Seconds a refresh token stays valid, 3600–7776000 (90 days).
# Defaults to 604800 (7 days).
#
# The real answer to "how long may somebody stay signed in", since every refresh
# issues a fresh one: a person who uses the application daily is never asked to
# log in again, and one who disappears for a week is.
# JWT_REFRESH_TTL=604800

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Account lifecycle — read by the activation and password-reset links, and by
# nothing else.
#
# New accounts are never emailed a password. An administrator creates the
# account, the backend emails a single-use link, and the person sets their own
# password through it. These three variables are what build that link and decide
# how long it lives.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# REQUIRED by the backend, and it has no default.
#
# Origin of the frontend the emailed links point at — scheme, host and optional
# port, with no path and no query. A trailing slash is tolerated and stripped.
#
# There is deliberately no fallback. A guessed value does not fail loudly: the
# invitation is sent successfully to a link nobody can open, and the symptom is
# a new colleague asking a week later why they never got in. In development this
# is the Vite dev server; in production it is wherever the frontend is served.
APP_WEB_URL=http://localhost:5173

# Optional. Seconds an activation link stays usable, 300–2592000 (30 days).
# Defaults to 259200 (72 hours).
#
# Long on purpose, and much longer than a reset. An invitation is sent whenever
# an administrator gets round to creating the account — routinely a Friday for
# somebody starting on Monday — and a link that died overnight would mean the
# first thing a new colleague does is ask for another. The account it opens has
# no password and no data, so the window costs little.
# ACCOUNT_ACTIVATION_TTL=259200

# Optional. Seconds a password-reset link stays usable, 300–86400 (24 hours).
# Defaults to 3600 (1 hour).
#
# Short, for the opposite reason. A reset link opens an account that already
# holds somebody's timesheets, their leave and possibly administrative
# authority, and the person who asked for it is at their keyboard now. Every
# extra hour is time the link spends in a mailbox being a working credential.
# ACCOUNT_PASSWORD_RESET_TTL=3600

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rate limiting — read by the backend's rate-limiting module, and by nothing
# else.
#
# All of it is optional, and an environment that says nothing about it still
# boots RATE LIMITED, with the defaults below — an absent variable is never
# read as "no limit".
#
# There are two tiers, and a request to an authentication route is counted in
# both:
#
#   * the BASELINE, on every route in the API. One bucket per client for the
#     whole API rather than one per route, which is what makes it a bound on the
#     request rate at all.
#   * the STRICT tier, on POST /auth/login and POST /auth/refresh only. Keyed on
#     the client address AND the submitted email address, so a whole office
#     behind one NAT address does not share ten attempts and one person mistyping
#     a password cannot lock out their colleagues.
#
# Both are starting points meant to be tuned per deployment: the right allowance
# depends on how many people use the system and from how many addresses, and
# neither is knowable from the code. That is the whole reason they are here
# rather than in it.
#
# A blocked client gets HTTP 429 with errorCode RATE_LIMIT_EXCEEDED and a
# Retry-After header giving the wait in seconds.
#
# NOTE: counters are held in memory, so they are per backend INSTANCE. Running
# more than one instance multiplies the effective limit by the instance count —
# see FEATURES/034-rate-limiting.md for the shared-store fix when that happens.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Optional. Requests one client may make to the whole API per window, 1-100000.
# Defaults to 300. At the default window that is five requests a second,
# sustained: far above what the frontend does, far below what a script does.
# RATE_LIMIT_DEFAULT_LIMIT=300

# Optional. The baseline window in seconds, 1-86400. Defaults to 60.
# RATE_LIMIT_DEFAULT_TTL=60

# Optional. Attempts allowed against an authentication route per window,
# 1-100000. Defaults to 10 — thirty times smaller than the baseline, because it
# bounds something thirty times more dangerous: an unauthenticated bcrypt per
# request, guessing at a password.
# RATE_LIMIT_AUTH_LIMIT=10

# Optional. The strict window in seconds, 1-86400. Defaults to 300 (5 minutes).
# RATE_LIMIT_AUTH_TTL=300

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reverse proxy — read by the backend at startup.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Optional. Whether Express should believe the X-Forwarded-For header, and how
# far. Defaults to `false` (trust nothing), which is correct on a developer
# machine and for any backend clients reach directly.
#
# READ THIS BEFORE DEPLOYING BEHIND A PROXY. This setting decides what the
# backend thinks a client's address is, which is what rate limiting counts
# against and what is recorded beside every issued refresh token. Both ways of
# getting it wrong are serious:
#
#   * LEFT AT false BEHIND A PROXY — every request appears to come from the load
#     balancer, so the entire internet shares one rate-limit bucket. The first
#     busy minute locks out the whole company, and every session is logged with
#     the proxy's address. A self-inflicted outage.
#   * SET TO true WITH NO PROXY — X-Forwarded-For is a request header, so the
#     CALLER writes it. Anyone can present a fresh address on every request and
#     get a fresh bucket each time. Rate limiting is not weakened, it is gone.
#
# There is no value that is safe in both topologies, which is why this is
# configuration rather than a default.
#
# Accepted values:
#   false                    trust nothing (the default)
#   1                        trust one proxy hop — THE RECOMMENDATION behind a
#                            single nginx, ALB or ingress. Stays correct when the
#                            proxy's own address changes, as managed load
#                            balancers' do.
#   2, 3, ...                that many hops, for a chain (CDN in front of an LB)
#   10.0.0.0/8,loopback      trust these addresses/subnets, or the presets
#                            loopback / linklocal / uniquelocal
#   true                     trust every hop — only safe when NOTHING can reach
#                            the backend except the proxy. Logged as a warning
#                            at startup.
#
# The backend refuses to start on a value outside these shapes, because Express
# would otherwise treat a typo as a hostname and resolve addresses in a way
# nobody intended.
# TRUST_PROXY=false

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SMTP — read by the backend's email module, and by nothing else.
#
# All of it is optional: with no mail server configured the backend still
# starts, `GET /api/v1/email/health` answers
# `{ "configured": false, "enabled": false, "connection": "NOT_CONFIGURED" }`,
# and any attempt to send is refused with a 500 rather than failing silently.
# That is the intended state on a machine that has no mail server.
#
# To actually send, five of them are required: SMTP_HOST, SMTP_PORT, SMTP_USER,
# SMTP_PASSWORD and SMTP_FROM_EMAIL. Miss one and the module reports itself as
# unconfigured — half a configuration is not a configuration. A variable set to
# nothing (`SMTP_HOST=`) counts as missing.
#
# These live here rather than in the database because they are a property of the
# deployment, not of the company: staging and production talk to different mail
# servers with different credentials, and SMTP_PASSWORD is a secret, which a
# table read by the application is not the place for.
#
# Verify a configuration with:
#   GET  /api/v1/email/health   — connects and authenticates, sends nothing
#   POST /api/v1/email/test     — { "email": "you@example.com" }
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Optional. Master switch. `false` makes the backend drop every message instead
# of sending it — notifications are silently skipped and the action that
# triggered them still succeeds. Set it on any environment that holds real
# employee addresses and must not mail them, such as a staging copy of
# production. `POST /api/v1/email/test` is the exception and refuses to run,
# because a test that sends nothing would be answering a question it did not
# ask. Defaults to true.
# SMTP_ENABLED=true

# Required to send. Hostname of the SMTP server.
# SMTP_HOST=smtp.example.com

# Required to send. Port, 1-65535. 587 for STARTTLS (the usual choice), 465 for
# implicit TLS, 1025 for a local Mailpit / MailHog.
# SMTP_PORT=587

# Optional. `true` opens the connection with TLS from the first byte, `false`
# starts in the clear and upgrades with STARTTLS. Only `true` and `false` are
# accepted. When unset it is derived from the port — true for 465, false for
# everything else — which is the right answer for every common server, so leave
# it alone unless yours is unusual.
# SMTP_SECURE=false

# Required to send. Account the backend authenticates as. For a provider like
# SendGrid this is a literal token name (`apikey`) rather than an address.
# SMTP_USER=no-reply@example.com

# Required to send. Password or API key for SMTP_USER. A secret — never commit
# it, and never reuse a person's mailbox password here.
# SMTP_PASSWORD=replace_with_smtp_password

# Optional. Display name shown to recipients, e.g. "HR Management System"
# <no-reply@example.com>. Without it the address appears on its own.
# SMTP_FROM_NAME=HR Management System

# Required to send. Address every message is sent from. Most providers require
# it to be a domain you have verified; a mismatch is a common cause of mail
# being accepted and then silently discarded.
# SMTP_FROM_EMAIL=no-reply@example.com

# Optional. Address replies should go to when that is not SMTP_FROM_EMAIL —
# typically a mailbox somebody reads, since the sending address usually is not.
# SMTP_REPLY_TO=hr@example.com

# Optional. Milliseconds to wait for the connection to the mail server, minimum
# 1000. Sending happens inside an HTTP request, so this bounds how long a
# request can be held open by an unreachable host. Defaults to 10000.
# SMTP_CONNECTION_TIMEOUT=10000

# Optional. Milliseconds an established connection may stay silent, minimum
# 1000. Covers the rest of the conversation, including a server that accepts the
# message and then thinks about it. Defaults to 30000.
# SMTP_SOCKET_TIMEOUT=30000

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Notification Delivery Engine — read by the notification-delivery module only.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Optional, defaults to true. Whether this deployment runs the engine's two
# scheduled jobs: the per-minute check for campaigns whose `scheduledAt` has
# arrived, and the daily check for reminder rules that are due.
#
# It does NOT switch the engine off — POST /api/v1/notification-delivery/execute
# /:campaignId still sends, because that is somebody deliberately asking. It
# stops the clock.
#
# Set it to false on any deployment holding a copy of production data. A staging
# database carries real employees, real addresses and real scheduled campaigns,
# and SMTP_ENABLED=false is not enough on its own: an in-app notification is
# delivered whether or not mail is configured.
#
# The WebSocket layer has no switch of its own and needs none — a gateway with
# nobody connected does nothing — and it reuses CORS_ORIGINS above rather than
# keeping a second allowlist.
# NOTIFICATION_SCHEDULER_ENABLED=true

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database seeding — read by `backend/prisma/seed.ts` only, never by the
# running application.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Optional. Password given to every account created by `npm run prisma:seed`.
# When unset, the seed uses the development default documented in
# `FEATURES/005-database-seeding.md`, which is published in this repository.
#
# Set it to anything else whenever the database is reachable by someone other
# than you — a shared development server, a demo environment, a teammate's
# machine. The seed refuses to run at all when NODE_ENV=production.
#
# Changing it only affects accounts the seed creates. Existing accounts keep
# their current password; `npm run prisma:reset` recreates them from scratch.
# SEED_PASSWORD=
